 "report": "Particle A42-spiral-e000-d011: knot (k=6)\n\nWhat it is: a particle of mass 4.3 and radius 1.52 with 6 ports on its rim. Each port is a stick-slip contact with a dead zone (mean 0.37) and a 4-dim charge; 4 of its port pairs attract each other strongly (so copies can bind port to port). Inside, 16 couplings (50% inhibitory) pass a push from port to port; it settles in 3 sweeps with slip rate 0.66 and keeps its state (memory).\nHow it assembles (32 copies, 2 worlds): 0.34 bonds per particle, largest molecule 2.5 particles, 21.5 left free.\n\nIts matter vs an ESN of the same size (readout only, both with tuned input scale); controls: no bonds, random bonds:\n  memcap   0.105 vs ESN 0.999\u00b10.000  [below]  no bonds 0.106  random bonds 0.110\n  xor      0.570 vs ESN 0.529\u00b10.011  [beat]  no bonds 0.608  random bonds 0.524\n  narma10  0.326 vs ESN 0.841\u00b10.002  [below]  no bonds 0.246  random bonds 0.320\n  latch    0.888 vs ESN 0.679\u00b10.009  [beat]  no bonds 0.885  random bonds 0.889\n  mnist14  0.493 vs ESN 0.816\u00b10.007  [below]  no bonds 0.494  random bonds 0.503\n\nGood at: xor, latch.\nIts assembly does not matter: the particles do as well unbonded, so the useful part is the single particle's own stick-slip memory.\nUse (a guess): combining recent events nonlinearly (temporal feature detection); holding a value until it is overwritten, with nothing trained but the readout: a physical memory element for a recurrent or reservoir system."
